<h1>Montez Press Radio Studio</h1>
<p><a href="https://radio.montezpress.com">Montez Press Radio</a> (MPR), an experimental broadcasting and performance platform on air since 2018, is moving from its current location, above a skate shop on Canal Street in Chinatown, NYC, to 215 Centre Street, a few blocks away, where it will share a building with the Museum of Chinese in America (MOCA) and several other cultural institutions. The building sits in a part of Chinatown that is changing fast: a new detention center is going up nearby as part of the city’s effort to close Rikers Island, and new tenants and long-standing communities are negotiating what the neighborhood will become. The studio takes on the design of MPR’s new space as a real commission with a real counterpart, and as a way to think about this condition at two scales: the build-out of MPR’s space itself, and the sites and streets around it.</p>
<p>The studio begins with a collective <a href="/precedents-report/">precedents report</a>: each student researches at least one case study and writes it as a vignette, and we edit the vignettes together into a single report, which goes on air from week 8 in six one-hour broadcasts on the studio’s own <a href="/radio/">radio station</a>. For the architectural project, we use a methodology developed, tested, and constantly updated by APRDELESP, through which we conceive architectural drawings as the workspace where the dialogue between everyone involved in a design process takes place. The project becomes the result of a conversation around social dynamics and material conditions, both existing and proposed: the drawing becomes a relationship rather than an object.</p>
